WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.423
Now the mechanism, because Conditional
Access doesn't work like Group Policy,

2
00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:09.138
and administrators who bring Group Policy
habits to it make predictable mistakes.

3
00:00:09.138 --> 00:00:10.550
There's no precedence.

4
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.524
Within a policy, assignments
combine with AND:

5
00:00:13.524 --> 00:00:17.750
the policy applies only when
everything it's assigned to matches.

6
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.975
Across policies, every applicable
policy must be satisfied.

7
00:00:21.975 --> 00:00:25.689
If one requires MFA and
another a compliant device,

8
00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:30.375
the user does both, and the policy
numbers are labels, not an order.

9
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:34.963
Block wins: if any applicable
policy blocks, enforcement stops.

10
00:00:34.963 --> 00:00:40.787
Inside a policy, controls combine as
configured, require all or require one.

11
00:00:40.787 --> 00:00:42.701
And exclusions are absolute.

12
00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:45.775
The rule that causes the
most damage is the default.

13
00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:49.913
If no policy's conditions trigger
a control, a token is issued.

14
00:00:49.913 --> 00:00:55.216
A policy that says Finance users reaching
Payroll must use phishing-resistant MFA

15
00:00:55.216 --> 00:00:57.215
says nothing about anyone else,

16
00:00:57.215 --> 00:01:01.288
so a non-Finance user gets a Payroll
token with no controls at all.

17
00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:06.261
Restricting a resource takes two
policies, written and reviewed together:

18
00:01:06.261 --> 00:01:09.950
a grant for the population,
and a block for everyone else.

19
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.971
Enterprise application assignment,
with Assignment required set,

20
00:01:13.971 --> 00:01:18.207
is usually the cleaner first gate,
but Global Administrators bypass it,

21
00:01:18.207 --> 00:01:21.725
which is why sensitive applications
keep the block as well.

22
00:01:21.725 --> 00:01:24.263
Two timing facts keep our claims honest.

23
00:01:24.263 --> 00:01:27.856
Conditional Access runs after
first-factor authentication,

24
00:01:27.856 --> 00:01:31.026
so it isn't a front-line
defense against password spray.

25
00:01:31.026 --> 00:01:35.239
And it evaluates token requests:
at sign-in, and when a refresh

26
00:01:35.239 --> 00:01:39.875
token gets a new access token, about
once an hour for modern clients.

27
00:01:39.875 --> 00:01:43.231
Continuous Access Evaluation
closes part of that gap,

28
00:01:43.231 --> 00:01:48.525
and supporting services enforce critical
events typically within fifteen minutes.

29
00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:51.500
With that model in
place, on to module one.
